Author: Theodore Nguyen-Cao <theo@guidedclinical.com>
Date:   Mon Aug 24 18:22:59 2026 -0400

    OR-2782: attribute the case to its anesthesia clinician at Anes Start
    
    Anesthesia Start owns the case's attribution and In Room only fills an absent
    one, so the two milestones settle on the same answer whichever order they
    arrive in.
    
    Who that clinician is comes from the case's AIP roster rather than the stored
    FHIR credential, because Mayo's Epic reports roughly half of Resident and
    Fellow as surgical, while AIP-4 records the role a person holds on this case,
    which is the question being asked. The roster is keyed on PERID: the previous
    lookup read client_user_id, a column nothing on this path writes, so it never
    once resolved a practitioner.
    
    The column is written by guarded statements rather than through the entity
    merge because two scheduling messages for one case are processed concurrently,
    and a read-then-write lets a stale "nobody attributed yet" land after the
    owning milestone has committed. OperationService.startOperation moves onto the
    same guarded statement for that reason; its fill-if-null semantics are
    unchanged for every tenant.
    
    A failure to resolve the highest-ranked clinician abandons the overwrite
    instead of falling through to a lower-ranked one, because the overwrite is
    unconditional and a replay would otherwise make that downgrade permanent.

